


{"id":"b189e3e8-eadb-466d-92d1-22829c4db52a","object":"chat.completion","created":1786443930,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_039\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_02\",\"depart_slot\":20,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_08\",\"depart_slot\":16,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_06\",\"depart_slot\":10,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_01\",\"depart_slot\":2,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_09\",\"depart_slot\":2,\"charge_blocks\":1}]}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task with a baseline and an intervention. The task: produce a repair covering every mission exactly once. We need to output decisions for each mission: mission_id, uav_id (must be the fixed one from mission assignment), held (boolean), path_id, depart_slot, charge_blocks.\n\nWe have two UAVs: UAV_01 and UAV_02, each assigned to specific missions with fixed order. The assignment_order_contract says \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\", meaning each UAV has its missions in a specific sequence. Looking at missions, they have sequence_index: For UAV_02, missions: MISSION_04 (seq 0), MISSION_02 (seq 1), MISSION_01 (seq 2). For UAV_01, missions: MISSION_05 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1). So we must serve them in that order per UAV. They could be held? The baseline has all missions not held. The intervention may include some overrides, and we need to repair respecting overriding constraints.\n\nWe need to check the energy, corridor capacities, pad capacities, charger capacities, closed corridors, and other constraints. The public task includes initial_soc_overrides: for UAV_01, initial_soc_kwh is 2.867135 (instead of 9.02689). That is a big reduction. For UAV_02, initial_soc is 2.978964 as given.\n\nWe need to respect the normative event order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. Incoming deadhead is given per mission: all missions have incoming_deadhead: null. So no deadhead energy cost. Service flight is the path energy. Then check reserve (reserve_kwh must remain after service flight). After turnaround (1 slot), post-service charging can happen. Charging can be up to max_charge_blocks=3, but charge_blocks is part of decision. It consumes charger slots at destination, and battery capacity is limited.\n\nLet's compute each UAV's mission sequence and energy constraints.\n\nFirst, get details:\n\n- Slot duration: slot_min = 5 minutes? Actually horizon_slots 41, slot_min 5 so 205 minutes. Not directly used perhaps.\n\n- Turnaround_slots: 1.\n\n- UAV_01: \n   - uav_type: fixed_wing_vtol_cargo_uav, battery_capacity_kwh: 22.0, charge_kwh_per_block: 0.95, empty_energy_kwh_per_km: 0.145, initial_soc_kwh: 2.867135 (overridden), reserve_kwh: 2.5, payload_capacity_kg: 7.0, payload_energy_kwh_per_km_kg: 0.012, max_wind_level: 2, travel_time_coefficient: 0.86, compatible layers: [\"L120_220\",\"L220_320\"].\n   - Missions assigned: MISSION_05 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1).\n   - For each mission, compute energy needed:\n        MISSION_05: origin VP_03, destination VP_05, payload 0.5 kg, release_slot 1, due_slot 7.\n           Path options: PATH_03 (COR_03, distance 33.6 km, travel_slots 7, wind_level 1, max_wind_level 1, energy_kwh 5.403384), PATH_07 (COR_05, distance 2.0 km, travel_slots 3, wind_level 0, max_wind_level 0, energy 0.302), PATH_09 (COR_04, distance 1.92 km, travel_slots 2, wind_level 1, max_wind_level 1, energy 0.308765).\n        MISSION_03: origin VP_05, destination VP_01, payload 0.5 kg, release_slot 10, due_slot 19.\n           Path option: PATH_06 (COR_02, distance 0.768 km, travel_slots 1, wind_level 1, max_wind_level 1, energy_kwh 0.123506).\n\n- UAV_02:\n   - uav_type: multirotor_cargo_uav, battery_capacity_kwh: 16.0, charge_kwh_per_block: 1.2, empty_energy_kwh_per_km: 0.1, initial_soc_kwh: 2.978964, reserve_kwh: 1.5, payload_capacity_kg: 4.0, payload_energy_kwh_per_km_kg: 0.026, max_wind_level: 1, travel_time_coefficient: 1.0, compatible layers: [\"L120_220\",\"L220_320\"].\n   - Missions assigned: MISSION_04 (seq 0), MISSION_02 (seq 1), MISSION_01 (seq 2).\n        MISSION_04: origin VP_03, destination VP_05, payload 0.5 kg, release_slot 1, due_slot 3.\n           Path: PATH_01 (COR_01, distance 1.05 km, travel_slots 2, wind_level 0, energy_kwh 0.11865).\n        MISSION_02: origin VP_05, destination VP_04, payload 0.5 kg, release_slot 4, due_slot 21.\n           Path options: PATH_04 (COR_08, distance 0.96 km, travel_slots 2, wind_level 1, energy 0.120413), PATH_05 (COR_06, distance 7.68 km, travel_slots 14, wind_level 0, energy 0.86784), PATH_08 (COR_07, distance 0.96 km, travel_slots 2, wind_level 1, energy 0.120413).\n        MISSION_01: origin VP_04, destination VP_02, payload 0.5 kg, release_slot 20, due_slot 24.\n           Path: PATH_02 (COR_09, distance 7.68 km, travel_slots 1, wind_level 1, energy_kwh 0.963302).\n\nCheck path compatibility with UAV max wind: UAV_01 max_wind 2, so all paths OK. UAV_02 max_wind 1, so paths with wind_level>1 not allowed. All given paths have max_wind_level <= 1, so OK.\n\nNow, we need to assign a depart_slot for each mission, and it must be after release_slot, and the flight must finish before due_slot? Actually due_slot is the deadline for arrival? Usually due_slot is the latest arrival slot. The timing: depart_slot + travel_slots (inclusive? Typically arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots - 1? Or depends on definition. In the baseline, let's check: MISSION_04 depart_slot 1, travel_slots 2, due_slot 3. If arrival slot is depart_slot + travel_slots? If travel_slots is the number of slots the flight occupies, then the arrival slot would be depart_slot + travel_slots - 1. So depart_slot=1, travel_slots=2 => arrival at slot 2? due_slot 3, so arrival on slot 2 is before due_slot 3. That works. MISSION_05 baseline depart_slot 1, path PATH_03 travel_slots 7, due_slot 7. If arrival slot = 1+7-1 = 7, due_slot 7, so on-time. MISSION_02 depart_slot 4, PATH_04 travel_slots 2, due_slot 21 => arrival 5, well before. MISSION_03 depart_slot 10, PATH_06 travel_slots 1, due_slot 19 => arrival slot 10? due 19, early. MISSION_01 depart_slot 20, PATH_02 travel_slots 1, due_slot 24 => arrival slot 20? due 24, early.\n\nSo arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots - 1. Must be <= due_slot. Release slot <= depart_slot.\n\nWe also have to consider energy: initial SOC, after deducting service flight energy, must remain >= reserve_kwh. Also check that total energy after adding charge blocks doesn't exceed battery capacity.\n\nFor each UAV, missions are sequential. The post-service charging after a mission can charge the battery. The charging occurs after turnaround (1 slot) at the destination. For subsequent mission, the UAV's available energy at its start is the energy after all previous charging (and previous missions' SOC after flight + charging, capped by capacity). We must ensure that at the start of each mission, there is enough energy to perform the flight and satisfy reserve after flight.\n\nNow, we have interventions:\n- initial_soc_overrides: UAV_01 initial_soc_kwh: 2.867135. Baseline was 9.02689, so much lower.\n- closed_corridor_slots: many closures for COR_08 and COR_07. COR_08 closed from slot 4 to 43 (i.e., practically all slots). COR_07 closed from slot 4 to 15.\n- pad_capacity_overrides: VP_03 pad capacity reduced to 1 at slot 1.\n- charger_capacity_overrides: none (empty list)\n- corridor_capacity_overrides: none\n- mission_window_overrides: none\n- regime: binding\n\nSo essentially, COR_08 is closed for all relevant times, so PATH_04 (which uses COR_08) cannot be used for any depart_slot that would have its edge segment occupying closed slots. COR_08 closure slots: 4 to 43 inclusive. So for MISSION_02, if we use PATH_04, travel_slots=2, so the flight would occupy slots from depart_slot to depart_slot+1 (since travel_slots=2). For the flight to be valid, the occupied slots must not include any closed slots for the corridor. Since COR_08 is closed from slot 4 to 43, if depart_slot is such that any of these slots fall in that range, it's invalid. So depart_slot must be such that the flight slots are either entirely before slot 4 (i.e., depart_slot + 1 <= 3 => depart_slot <= 2) or entirely after slot 43 (depart_slot >= 44, but horizon is 41). So only depart_slot <= 2 would work, but MISSION_02 release slot is 4, so depart_slot >=4. Thus PATH_04 is impossible. MISSION_02 cannot use PATH_04 due to closure.\n\nSimilarly, COR_07 is closed from slot 4 to 15. PATH_08 uses COR_07, travel_slots=2. To avoid closure, depart_slot must be such that flight slots don't overlap closed ones. Release slot 4, so depart_slot >=4. If depart_slot=4, flight slots 4-5. COR_07 closed slots 4-15 -> overlapping, invalid. Same for any depart_slot up to? Flight slots are depart_slot and depart_slot+1. To avoid closed, we need both slots <4 or both >15. Since release 4, <4 not possible, >15 means depart_slot >= 15? If depart_slot=15, flight slots 15-16: slot 15 is closed, slot 16 is not closed? Closed list for COR_07 is 4 to 15 inclusive, so slot 15 closed, invalid. depart_slot=16 gives slots 16-17, both >15, so valid. So depart_slot must be >=16. That's allowed because due_slot is 21, arrival slot = depart_slot+1, so if depart_slot=16, arrival=17 <=21, ok. So PATH_08 is possible but only with depart_slot>=16.\n\nPATH_05 (COR_06) travel_slots 14, no closure. So available.\n\nSo for MISSION_02, we have PATH_05 and PATH_08 (with late depart). PATH_05 energy 0.86784, PATH_08 energy 0.120413 (same as PATH_04). So energy-efficient wise, PATH_08 is much better, but it forces a late departure. We need to consider UAV_02's sequence: MISSION_04 then MISSION_02 then MISSION_01. MISSION_01 release 20, due 24. MISSION_02 must finish before MISSION_01 start? Typically, a UAV can only do one mission at a time, and after turnaround and charging, it can start next. We need to check that the schedule is feasible: MISSION_02 must finish and then after turnaround (1 slot) and possibly charging, UAV_02 can depart for MISSION_01. Given MISSION_01 release 20, and due 24, MISSION_02 should finish early enough to allow that charging if needed. Let's see.\n\nSimilarly, UAV_01: MISSION_05 then MISSION_03. With low initial SOC, charging may be necessary.\n\nNow pad capacity overrides: VP_03 pad capacity = 1 at slot 1. That means at slot 1, there can be only one UAV using a pad at VP_03 (either departure or arrival). Our missions: MISSION_04 (UAV_02) origin VP_03, depart_slot baseline 1; MISSION_05 (UAV_01) origin VP_03, depart_slot baseline 1. Both would depart from VP_03 at slot 1, using pad capacity of 2 normally, but now only 1 at slot 1, so they can't both depart at slot 1. So we need to adjust depart slots to avoid exceeding pad capacity at slot 1. Also, if any arrival at VP_03 at slot 1, that would also consume pad capacity. No mission has destination VP_03? MISSION_04 destination VP_05, MISSION_05 destination VP_05, others not VP_03. So only departures from VP_03 for these two at slot 1 might conflict. So at most one of them can depart at slot 1. They could depart at different slots. Also, MISSION_04's release slot is 1, so can't depart earlier; due_slot 3, so must depart such that arrival by 3. Travel_slots 2, so depart_slot must be 1 or 2? If depart_slot=2, arrival slot=3 (due_slot=3), allowed. So MISSION_04 could depart at slot 2. MISSION_05 release 1, due 7, travel_slots for its chosen path; for PATH_03 travel_slots 7, depart_slot=1 gives arrival=7, OK; for PATH_07 travel_slots 3, depart_slot could be up to 5 (arrival 7); for PATH_09 travel_slots 2, depart_slot could be up to 6 (arrival 7). So there is flexibility.\n\nNow energy: UAV_01 initial SOC 2.867135. It must perform MISSION_05 and MISSION_03. MISSION_03 energy 0.123506, very low. So the main concern is MISSION_05. Path options: PATH_03 requires 5.403384 kWh, which is >2.867135, so not directly possible without charging before flight. But charging can only occur after a mission's turnaround, not before the first mission. At the start of MISSION_05, the UAV is at VP_03 with initial SOC. No charging occurred before because it's the first mission. So energy available is 2.867135. To fly MISSION_05, the energy consumed must leave at least reserve_kwh (2.5) after flight. So pre-flight SOC must be >= flight energy + reserve. So we need initial SOC >= energy + reserve. For PATH_03: 5.403384 + 2.5 = 7.903384, much larger. For PATH_07: 0.302 + 2.5 = 2.802, which is <= 2.867135? 2.867135 - 2.802 = 0.065135, so it works. For PATH_09: 0.308765 + 2.5 = 2.808765 <= 2.867135, so also works. So MISSION_05 cannot use PATH_03; must use either PATH_07 or PATH_09. PATH_07 energy 0.302, PATH_09 energy 0.308765. PATH_07 travel_slots 3, PATH_09 travel_slots 2. Both wind level 0/1 respectively, max_wind_level 0 for PATH_07, 1 for PATH_09; UAV_01 max_wind 2, both ok. So we'll pick the better one; PATH_09 has shorter travel time (2 slots) and slightly higher energy but still ok. However, we also need to consider departure pad capacity at VP_03. If both UAV_01 and UAV_02 want to depart VP_03 at slot 1, only one allowed. We can shift one.\n\nNow after MISSION_05, UAV_01 arrives at VP_05, turnaround 1 slot, then can charge. Then it must later perform MISSION_03 from VP_05 to VP_01. MISSION_03 energy 0.123506, so after possible charging, it must have at least 0.123506 + 2.5 = 2.623506 kWh. So the SOC after post-service charging (before departure of MISSION_03) must be >= 2.623506. After MISSION_05 flight, SOC = initial - flight energy. Using PATH_09: 2.867135 - 0.308765 = 2.55837. Reserve check: after flight, reserve must be satisfied, which is 2.5, and 2.55837 >= 2.5, ok. Then turnaround (1 slot). Then charging: we can add charge_blocks, each block adds 0.95 kWh, up to max 3 blocks, but total cannot exceed battery capacity 22.0. So after charging, SOC = min(2.55837 + n*0.95, 22.0). To reach at least 2.623506, we need n such that 2.55837 + n*0.95 >= 2.623506 => n*0.95 >= 0.065136 => n >= 0.0685, so n=1 is sufficient. So we'll need at least 1 charge block after MISSION_05 to have enough for MISSION_03 (reserve check after MISSION_03 flight). Actually reserve check is after service flight, so pre-flight SOC must be >= flight energy + reserve. So for MISSION_03, required pre-flight SOC = 0.123506 + 2.5 = 2.623506. So with 1 charge block after MISSION_05, SOC becomes 2.55837 + 0.95 = 3.50837, plenty. So charge_blocks for MISSION_05 decision could be 1 or more. But we also need to consider charger capacity at VP_05 and time constraints. Charger slots: each block consumes one slot? The charging occurs after turnaround, so charge_blocks indicates number of blocks, each block takes 1 slot? The standard is that a charge_block is 1 slot of charging (5 min maybe), and the UAV remains at the vertiport occupying a charger during those slots. So post-service charge consumes charger slots. Also, the charging slots must not overlap with pad usage? Usually charging happens at a charger, separate from pad. The capacity constraints: charger_capacity_by_slot_default is 2 for VP_05. So at any slot, at most 2 UAVs charging at VP_05. Also, charging occurs after turnaround, which means the UAV occupies a parking/charging spot. The timing: after service flight arrival at slot arr, turnaround takes 1 slot, so the UAV is available for charging from slot arr+1? Actually typical: arrival at slot t, then turnaround slots (1) occurs, so post-service charge can start at slot t+1. The charge_blocks then occupy slots from t+1 to t+charge_blocks. The UAV then departs for next mission at depart_slot which must be after charging completed (i.e., depart_slot >= t+charge_blocks+1? Or if turnaround is included? Need to see the exact event order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So service flight ends at arrival slot. Then turnaround for 1 slot. Then post-service charge for charge_blocks slots. Then the UAV is ready to depart for next mission. So if arrival slot is a, turnaround occupies slot a+1 (if we assume turnaround takes next slot). Then charging starts at slot a+2 and occupies slots a+2 ... a+1+charge_blocks. The UAV can depart for next mission at slot >= a+2+charge_blocks. But note that the next mission's departure slot cannot be before the charging ends. Also, charging must not collide with other UAVs' charging. We'll have to check these.\n\nNow UAV_02: initial SOC 2.978964. MISSION_04: PATH_01 energy 0.11865, reserve 1.5, so pre-flight needed 1.61865, initial 2.978964 >= that, ok. After flight, SOC = 2.978964 - 0.11865 = 2.860314. Reserve check: 2.860314 >= 1.5, ok. Then turnaround 1 slot, then possible charging. For MISSION_02 (next), we need energy: if we choose PATH_05 energy 0.86784, then needed pre-flight for MISSION_02 = 0.86784 + 1.5 = 2.36784. Current SOC after MISSION_04 is 2.860314, which is >= 2.36784, so no charging needed for MISSION_02. But if we choose PATH_08 energy 0.120413, then pre-flight needed = 1.620413, also satisfied. So after MISSION_04, no charging is necessary for energy. However, we might still want to charge to prepare for MISSION_01. MISSION_01 energy 0.963302, reserve 1.5, pre-flight needed = 2.463302. After MISSION_02, SOC = previous SOC - MISSION_02 energy. If we had SOC after MISSION_04 = 2.860314. Then after MISSION_02: if PATH_05: SOC = 2.860314 - 0.86784 = 1.992474. Reserve check: 1.992474 >= 1.5, ok. But for MISSION_01, pre-flight needed 2.463302, and we only have 1.992474, insufficient. So we must charge either after MISSION_04 or after MISSION_02 to have enough for MISSION_01. So we need to plan charging. Charging after any mission adds 1.2 kWh per block, battery capacity 16.0.\n\nIf we charge after MISSION_04: max 3 blocks. Say we add 1 block: SOC becomes 2.860314 + 1.2 = 4.060314 (but capped at 16). Then after MISSION_02 with PATH_05: SOC = 4.060314 - 0.86784 = 3.192474, which is >= 2.463302, good. Or with PATH_08: SOC after MISSION_02 = 4.060314 - 0.120413 = 3.939901, ok. So charging after MISSION_04 can help. Alternatively, charge after MISSION_02: after MISSION_02 (without prior charge) with PATH_05, SOC=1.992474, then add blocks: need 2.463302, so need at least 0.470828 kWh, so 1 block (1.2) gives 3.192474, enough. So charging after MISSION_02 works. But we must consider timing: MISSION_02 due 21, release 4, we need to fit charging before MISSION_01 release 20? Actually MISSION_01 can start after MISSION_02's charging, but MISSION_01 has its own release slot 20, so it cannot depart before slot 20. So if charging after MISSION_02 finishes after slot 20, it's ok as long as depart_slot >= 20 and >= charging end + ? Actually the order: after MISSION_02 arrival, turnaround, then post-service charge. That charge happens at VP_04 (MISSION_02 destination). MISSION_01 origin is VP_04, so the UAV is already at VP_04. So after charging, it can start MISSION_01. So depart_slot for MISSION_01 must be after the charging completes. So we need to schedule such that MISSION_01 can depart on or after release 20 and <= due 24, and that charging doesn't conflict with corridor/pad capacities.\n\nNow corridor closures: COR_08 closed entirely, so PATH_04 not allowed. For PATH_08, we need depart_slot >=16. So if we use PATH_08, MISSION_02 departure must be >=16, which means it could finish at slot 16+travel_slots=2? Actually travel_slots=2 for PATH_08, so if depart_slot=16, arrival slot = 16+2-1=17. Then turnaround slot 18. Then post-service charge at VP_04 could happen. Charging would start slot 19, could go if we want. MISSION_01 release 20, so depart_slot could be 20 if charging done by slot 19? If charge_blocks=0, no charging, depart_slot could be 20 directly (since turnaround ends at 18, so ready at 19? Wait: arrival slot 17, turnaround slot 18 (occupied 1 slot), then the UAV is free at slot 19. So depart_slot for MISSION_01 could be 19 or later, but release is 20, so earliest 20. So that works: depart MISSION_01 at 20. No charging needed after MISSION_02 if we had enough energy from prior charge. But if we used PATH_08, after MISSION_02, SOC remaining is from earlier SOC after MISSION_04 plus possible charging minus PATH_08 energy. If we didn't charge after MISSION_04, SOC after MISSION_04=2.860314, after PATH_08=2.739901. That's still less than 2.463302. Actually 2.739901 >= 2.463302, so no charging needed at all for MISSION_01! Let's recalc: Pre-flight for MISSION_01 needed = energy + reserve = 0.963302 + 1.5 = 2.463302. So SOC after MISSION_02 must be >= 2.463302. If we take PATH_08 with no charging after MISSION_04: SOC after MISSION_04 = 2.860314, after PATH_08 = 2.860314 - 0.120413 = 2.739901, which is > 2.463302. So it's sufficient. So no charging needed at all for UAV_02 if we use PATH_08 for MISSION_02. However, PATH_08 forces late MISSION_02 (depart>=16). Then MISSION_01 can start at 20. This seems feasible. But check: MISSION_02 release slot 4, so we could depart at 16, that's allowed. But also we must account for pad capacity and other constraints.\n\nNow the pad capacity at VP_03 at slot 1 is 1. So we need to avoid both MISSION_04 and MISSION_05 departing at slot 1. In baseline, both departed at 1. We can shift one. MISSION_04 (UAV_02) depart_slot can be 2 (arrival slot 3, due 3 ok). MISSION_05 (UAV_01) using PATH_09 (travel_slots 2) could depart at slot 2 as well? Or depart at slot 2 gives arrival 3? Actually if depart_slot=2, travel_slots=2 => arrival slot = 2+2-1=3. That's before due 7, ok. But pad capacity at VP_03 for slot 1 would be 1, but if both depart at slot 2, pad capacity at slot 2 is default 2, so that's fine. Also MISSION_04 release 1, can depart at 2. So we can set MISSION_04 depart_slot=2, MISSION_05 depart_slot=2. But then both use VP_03 at slot 2: pad capacity is 2, ok. However, MISSION_05 using PATH_09: wind_level 1, max_wind 1; UAV_02 has max_wind 1, so that's ok. But we need to check corridor capacity: COR_04 (PATH_09) and COR_01 (PATH_01) might be used at same time? MISSION_04 uses COR_01 (VP_03->VP_05), travel_slots 2, depart_slot 2 => flight slots 2-3. MISSION_05 uses COR_04 (VP_03->VP_05)? Actually PATH_09 goes VP_03 to VP_05 via COR_04, not COR_01. So different corridors, so no conflict. Pad is at VP_03, two separate pads maybe, capacity 2, so fine.\n\nNow check UAV_01: after MISSION_05 (depart 2, arrival 3), turnaround slot 4, then charge. MISSION_03 release 10, due 19, origin VP_05, destination VP_01. We need to have enough SOC after charging. After MISSION_05 PATH_09, SOC=2.55837, charge blocks needed to reach at least 2.623506. One block gives 3.50837, so we'll add charge_blocks=1 after MISSION_05. That charging occupies slots: arrival slot 3, turnaround slot 4, charging slot 5 (since charge_blocks=1, occupies slot 5). Then MISSION_03 can depart at slot >= 6? But release slot 10, so earliest 10. So we can set depart_slot for MISSION_03 = 10. That fits. Check charging slot capacity at VP_05: charger_count 2, slot 5, no other charging at that time? Only UAV_01 is charging there. UAV_02 will not be at VP_05 at that time: MISSION_04 arrives at VP_05 at slot 3 (depart 2, travel 2 => arrival 3). Then turnaround slot 4, then it could charge or just wait. In our plan for UAV_02, we used no charging after MISSION_04. So UAV_02 after turnaround at VP_05 would just idle. But it might need to depart for MISSION_02 from VP_05. MISSION_02 origin VP_05. So after turnaround (slot 4), UAV_02 is free at slot 5? Actually turnaround occupies slot 4, so it's free at slot 5. But MISSION_02 release slot 4, so it could depart as early as slot 5? Wait: turnaround ends at slot 4 (arrival 3, turnaround 1 slot -> slot 4). So UAV_02 is ready to depart at slot 5. But MISSION_02 release is 4, so it can depart at slot 5 or later. However, if we choose PATH_08 for MISSION_02, due to corridor closure we need depart_slot >=16. So UAV_02 will wait at VP_05 from slot 5 until slot 16. That's idle time, but allowed. During that time, it occupies a pad? The pad is used for departure only at departure slot. Idle time might use a parking spot? The instance does not explicitly limit parking, only pad and charger capacities. So as long as it doesn't violate pad capacity at the departure slot, it's fine. So UAV_02 can idle at VP_05.\n\nNow, check charger capacity at VP_05: UAV_01 is charging at slot 5. UAV_02 is not charging, so ok.\n\nNow UAV_02 MISSION_02: using PATH_08, depart_slot=16, travel_slots=2 => arrives at VP_04 at slot 17. Turnaround slot 18. Then it's ready at slot 19. MISSION_01 release 20, depart_slot 20 from VP_04. Travel_slots 1 => arrival slot 20 (due 24 ok). Check energy: initial SOC 2.978964. After MISSION_04 (PATH_01): 2.978964 - 0.11865 = 2.860314. No charging. After MISSION_02 (PATH_08): 2.860314 - 0.120413 = 2.739901. After reserve check: always >=1.5. Then after MISSION_01 (PATH_02): 2.739901 - 0.963302 = 1.776599, reserve 1.5 ok. So energy works without any charging for UAV_02.\n\nCheck corridor capacities and closures: MISSION_01 uses COR_09 (VP_04->VP_02) travel_slots 1, depart_slot 20 -> flight slot 20. COR_09 not closed, capacity default 2, ok. MISSION_02 PATH_08 uses COR_07, travel_slots 2, depart_slot 16 -> slots 16-17. COR_07 closure: slots 4-15 closed, so slots 16-17 not closed, ok. capacity 2. MISSION_04 PATH_01 uses COR_01, depart_slot 2 -> slots 2-3. No closure, ok. MISSION_05 PATH_09 uses COR_04, depart_slot 2 -> slots 2-3. No closure, ok. MISSION_03 PATH_06 uses COR_02, depart_slot 